<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Koliya republic</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 450 BCE (approx.) · capital Ramagrama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small clan republic in the Himalayan foothills, home of the Buddha's mother and neighbour and kin of the Shakyas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Koliyas lived east of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakya republic</a>, across the Rohini river in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They emerged as a self-governing clan by the 7th or 6th century BCE, with their main town at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramagrama">Ramagrama</a>. The two clans intermarried, and Buddhist tradition makes Maya, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">the Buddha</a>, a Koliya princess.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakyas</a>, the Koliyas were governed by an assembly of clan heads. They grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> on land watered by the Rohini, and Buddhist texts tell how a quarrel with the Shakyas over its water nearly led to war until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">the Buddha</a> reconciled them. They were dependent on the larger <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kingdom of Kosala</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">the Buddha</a>'s death the Koliyas received a share of his relics and built a stupa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramagrama">Ramagrama</a>, which survives in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>. They were absorbed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kosala</a> and then by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> in the 5th century BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 650 BCE – 450 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.77%;width:3.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 450 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ramagrama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Koliyas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=koliya-republic" aria-label="Koliya republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6880 1135 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Koliya republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7266 1513h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7200 1391h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7200 1391h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Nepal. <a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=koliya-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

